  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Start the rice: rinse it, cook it with 1 1/2 cups water, covered, over the lowest heat for 15 minutes, then rest it off the heat.",
      "timer_min": 15
    },
    {
      "text": "Heat the oil in a large skillet over medium-high. Add the beef and cook 5-6 minutes, breaking it small with a spoon, until it loses its red color. Ground beef is safe at 160°F (71°C) — check a clump on a thermometer.",
      "timer_min": 6,
      "safety": {
        "class": "meat_ground",
        "temp_f": 160,
        "method": "thermometer",
        "source": "USDA FSIS safe minimum internal temperature table"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Spoon off all but a tablespoon of fat. Add the onion and cook 4 minutes, softening it in the drippings.",
      "timer_min": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Add the garlic, cumin, oregano, and bay leaf for 30 seconds, then the tomatoes, olives, and raisins with their brines and half the salt."
    },
    {
      "text": "Simmer 12 minutes, uncovered, stirring once or twice, until the sauce is dark and thick enough to hold a spoon-drag.",
      "timer_min": 12
    },
    {
      "text": "Pull the bay leaf, season with the rest of the salt and the pepper, and serve the picadillo over rice — sweet, salty, and briny all at once."
    }
  ],
  "cuisine_note": "Cuban picadillo runs on contrast: olives for salt, raisins for sweet, cumin for warmth. The same pot feeds tacos, empanada filling, or next week's freezer — it freezes beautifully.",
